From: Fanha Giang <fanha99@hotmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: dsa: tag_8021q: give a port's VLAN only to its own CPU port

dsa_port_tag_8021q_vlan_match() makes every CPU port a member of every
tag_8021q VLAN, while a bridge VLAN is given to the targeted port's own
CPU port alone (dsa_port_host_vlan_match()). On a switch with two CPU
ports that difference costs the second conduit everything it was meant
to add.

A standalone port's traffic crosses the conduit in that port's tag_8021q
VLAN. Unknown unicast is flooded to every CPU port - GLOBAL_FW_CTRL1 on
a QCA8337, the equivalent elsewhere - and the far end of a standalone
port is never in the FDB, because DSA keeps learning off on such a port.
So every frame routed out of a standalone port is an unknown-unicast
flood, and because the tag_8021q VLAN spans both CPU ports, a copy of it
reaches the second one as well. The copies are then dropped by the host
with rx_dropped, rx_errors and mmc_rx_fifo_overflow all at zero, so
nothing in the statistics says where the throughput went.

Measured on a Xiaomi CR8806 (IPQ5018 + QCA8337, two CPU ports), routed
and NAT through the board, 20 s, 4 streams. With the second CPU port
merely enabled - idle, owning no user port, all four user ports on the
other conduit - it receives a copy of the whole ingress stream
(1,675,825 frames against 1,676,403 on the conduit), throughput falls
932 -> 723 Mbit/s and cpu0 softirq goes 0.3% -> 98.3%. A second session
on a fresh boot reproduced the copy at 1,687,067 against 1,677,515 with
cpu0 softirq 91%, and the switch registers read mid-load were identical
to idle, so the load reprograms nothing. With this patch and nothing
else changed: 933 Mbit/s, cpu0 softirq 2%, and zero frames on the second
CPU port for the whole run.

Injecting unknown unicast directly makes the before and after explicit.
Pointing a permanent neighbour entry at an unowned MAC on a standalone
port and sending 20 fragmented pings gives ~820 flooded frames: before,
the second CPU port received 825 of them; after, 0 on each of three
runs, while GLOBAL_FW_CTRL1, the port's PORT_LOOKUP_CTRL and the VLAN
itself were unchanged.

The second conduit then does what it is for. With the WAN port on one
conduit and the LAN ports on the other, a routed frame crosses one
conduit per direction instead of the same conduit twice: the two
conduits carry equal and complementary loads (1050/430 against 430/1056
Mbit/s), a single conduit's hard ceiling of 927-929 Mbit/s bidirectional
becomes 1104-1458 over three runs, and the NSS flow offload accelerates
normally where before it installed no rule at all and reported
src_mismatch_fail_count against every attempt. The spread in the second
figure is the test rig - one 1 GbE host on each side, so an aggregate
number needs iperf3 --bidir, whose scheduling varies widely between runs
of the same configuration; each direction measured on its own reaches
945 Mbit/s, the wire rate of the port.

Keeping the VLAN on one CPU port means it has to follow the port when
the port's conduit changes at runtime, which is what the two helpers
added to dsa_port_change_conduit() do. A bridged port needs nothing
extra there: its VLAN is handled by the bridge leave and rejoin that
function already does around the switch. Exercised by moving a port
between the two conduits repeatedly at runtime.

Signed-off-by: Fanha Giang <fanha99@hotmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/net/dsa/tag_8021q.c
+++ b/net/dsa/tag_8021q.c
@@ -207,11 +207,24 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* Tag_8021q VLANs match on the targeted port, on all DSA ports, and - as host
+ * VLANs do, see dsa_port_host_vlan_match() - on the targeted port's own CPU
+ * port alone. Giving them to every CPU port instead makes the switch flood a
+ * standalone port's traffic to the other CPU ports as well: the flood domain
+ * of an unknown unicast is every CPU port, and a standalone port's far end is
+ * never in the FDB because learning is off on such a port. The copies are
+ * then dropped by the host, at the cost of a core and of the throughput the
+ * second conduit was supposed to add.
+ */
 static bool
 dsa_port_tag_8021q_vlan_match(struct dsa_port *dp,
 			      struct dsa_notifier_tag_8021q_vlan_info *info)
 {
-	return dsa_port_is_dsa(dp) || dsa_port_is_cpu(dp) || dp == info->dp;
+	if (dsa_port_is_cpu(dp))
+		return info->dp->type == DSA_PORT_TYPE_USER &&
+		       info->dp->cpu_dp == dp;
+
+	return dsa_port_is_dsa(dp) || dp == info->dp;
 }
 
 int dsa_switch_tag_8021q_vlan_add(struct dsa_switch *ds,
@@ -386,6 +399,27 @@
 	vlan_vid_del(conduit, ctx->proto, vid);
 }
 
+/* A port's standalone VLAN is programmed on its own CPU port, so it has to
+ * follow the port when the port's conduit changes. Call the first of these
+ * while dp->cpu_dp still points at the old CPU port, and the second once it
+ * points at the new one. A bridged port's VLAN needs nothing here: it is
+ * handled by the bridge leave and rejoin that dsa_port_change_conduit() does
+ * around the switch.
+ */
+void dsa_tag_8021q_port_pre_change_conduit(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port)
+{
+	if (ds->tag_8021q_ctx)
+		dsa_tag_8021q_port_teardown(ds, port);
+}
+
+int dsa_tag_8021q_port_post_change_conduit(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port)
+{
+	if (!ds->tag_8021q_ctx)
+		return 0;
+
+	return dsa_tag_8021q_port_setup(ds, port);
+}
+
 static int dsa_tag_8021q_setup(struct dsa_switch *ds)
 {
 	int err, port;
--- a/net/dsa/tag_8021q.h
+++ b/net/dsa/tag_8021q.h
@@ -25,4 +25,7 @@
 int dsa_switch_tag_8021q_vlan_del(struct dsa_switch *ds,
 				  struct dsa_notifier_tag_8021q_vlan_info *info);
 
+void dsa_tag_8021q_port_pre_change_conduit(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port);
+int dsa_tag_8021q_port_post_change_conduit(struct dsa_switch *ds, int port);
+
 #endif
--- a/net/dsa/port.c
+++ b/net/dsa/port.c
@@ -1469,9 +1469,21 @@
 	if (dev->flags & IFF_UP)
 		dsa_user_host_uc_uninstall(dev);
 
+	/* The port's standalone tag_8021q VLAN lives on its own CPU port, so it
+	 * has to be moved across together with the port.
+	 */
+	dsa_tag_8021q_port_pre_change_conduit(ds, dp->index);
+
 	err = dsa_port_assign_conduit(dp, conduit, extack, true);
 	if (err)
-		goto rewind_old_addrs;
+		goto rewind_old_tag_8021q;
+
+	err = dsa_tag_8021q_port_post_change_conduit(ds, dp->index);
+	if (err) {
+		NL_SET_ERR_MSG_MOD(extack,
+				   "Failed to migrate the standalone VLAN");
+		goto rewind_addr_inherit;
+	}
 
 	/* If the port doesn't have its own MAC address and relies on the DSA
 	 * conduit's one, inherit it again from the new DSA conduit.
@@ -1527,10 +1539,15 @@
 	if (is_zero_ether_addr(dp->mac))
 		eth_hw_addr_inherit(dev, old_conduit);
 
+	dsa_tag_8021q_port_pre_change_conduit(ds, dp->index);
+
 	dsa_port_assign_conduit(dp, old_conduit, NULL, false);
 
-/* Restore the objects on the old CPU port */
-rewind_old_addrs:
+/* Put the standalone VLAN back on the old CPU port */
+rewind_old_tag_8021q:
+	dsa_tag_8021q_port_post_change_conduit(ds, dp->index);
+
+	/* Restore the objects on the old CPU port */
 	if (dev->flags & IFF_UP) {
 		tmp = dsa_user_host_uc_install(dev, dev->dev_addr);
 		if (tmp) {
